    {
      "id": "library/9789240081291-eng/blocks/prose-sec-003",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "What is new?",
      "pageStart": 12,
      "pageEnd": 15,
      "target": "../sections/sec-003-what-is-new.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "This revision of the NHWA incorporates several aspects beyond data and indicators: the \nevolution of the global health agenda since 2017, new indicators needed and revision of existing \nindicators, the evolution of the occupations’ definitions, new tools and guidance.\nThe global health agenda has evolved drastically since the launch of NHWA, with the COVID-19 \npandemic hitting the world in 2020 (1). In addition to other recent emerging events with direct \nimpact on health and healthcare – such as armed conflict, ecological crisis, zoonotic diseases \n– the global COVID-19 pandemic called for ac",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "What is new in the second edition. The global health agenda changed after the COVID-19 pandemic, raising the need to strengthen the public health and emergency workforce, addressed by a 2022 Roadmap for the essential public health functions. A projected shortage of 10 million health and care workers by 2030 remains, and 181 countries, territories and areas now have a nominated NHWA focal point. New initiatives (the Working for Health programme, the Global Strategic Directions on Nursing and Midwifery, CHW guidance, the Global Health and Care Worker Compact) needed monitoring, and focal points and experts asked for fewer indicators. Version 2 therefore has four modules and 29 indicators: of version 1's 78 indicators, 62 were kept (often merged or turned into disaggregations or composite capacity indicators), 16 were dropped and 2 added — health workforce education and training capacity, and share of women in leadership role — retaining about 80% of version 1's information (Figure 1), with bridging tables to ease the transition. The section is inconsistent with itself here: Figure 1's per-module counts of dropped indicators (1, 2, 2, 4, 2, 2, 3, 1, 0, 0 across v1 modules 1–10) add up to 17, not the 16 dropped and 62 maintained stated in the text and the figure caption. The occupation list was revised and expanded along ISCO-08, and the focal-point data platform and public portal were updated.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "claude-opus-5-5"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-31"
      }
