      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Global context on health and care workers",
      "pageStart": 16,
      "pageEnd": 17,
      "target": "../sections/sec-005-global-context-on-health-and-care-workers.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Global context on health and care workers\nA health workforce (HWF) of adequate size and skill mix is critical to the attainment of any \npopulation health goal. This includes the achievement of universal health coverage (UHC) (8) \nand the health-related targets of the United Nations Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs). \nYet countries globally are affected by multifaceted challenges, such as difficulties in HWF \neducation and training, deployment, performance and retention (9). Suboptimal allocation of \nhealth workers is one of the main challenges that directly influences the availability, acce",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Global context on health and care workers: a health workforce of adequate size and skill mix is critical to UHC and the health-related SDGs, yet countries face challenges in education, deployment, performance and retention, and maldistribution limits access to services. The Global Strategy on Human Resources for Health: Workforce 2030, adopted in May 2016 by resolution WHA69.19, sets objectives including stronger HRH data, with milestones for 2020 and 2030, and stresses strengthened national HRH information systems. The High-Level Commission on Health Employment and Economic Growth's report *Working for health and growth* set out 10 recommendations and a five-year action plan supported by ILO, OECD and WHO, leading to the joint Working for Health programme in 2017 and a call for harmonized health workforce data and metrics, which NHWA supports. COVID-19 (2020–2023) showed the workforce's central role: pulse surveys found shortages of health workers to be the most common cause of service disruptions and the biggest bottleneck to COVID-19 tools, underlining the need for a multisectoral workforce able to deliver the essential public health functions.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
