    {
      "id": "library/9789240081291-eng/blocks/prose-sec-007",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Applications at the global level",
      "pageStart": 17,
      "pageEnd": 19,
      "target": "../sections/sec-007-applications-at-the-global-level.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "From the Action Plan of the High-Level Commission, and specifically the GSHRH, the concept of \nNHWA was clearly promoted as an important means to support countries in their national HWF \npolicy and planning. The Sixty-ninth World Health Assembly adopted resolution WHA69.19 in \nsupport of its implementation (11). The purpose of the NHWA is to facilitate the standardization \nof HRHIS for interoperability, i.e. the ability to exchange HWF data within broader subnational \nor national health information systems (HIS), as well as within international databases. In \nthis way, rapid aggregation and di",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Applications at the global level: promoted by the High-Level Commission's action plan and the Global Strategy and supported by resolution WHA69.19, NHWA aims to standardize HRH information systems for interoperability so health workforce data can be exchanged and aggregated for decision-making. Since 2017, data availability and quality have improved greatly; as of April 2023, 181 countries, territories and areas have national focal points reporting annually on the NHWA data platform, and the public data portal has over 4,800 registered users. NHWA data underpin many knowledge products (Figure 2) and monitor global frameworks — the SDGs, UHC tracking, GPW13, the WHO Global Code of Practice on international recruitment, the nursing and midwifery strategic directions, the PHC measurement framework, Working for Health, the Health and Care Worker Compact, Immunization Agenda 2030 and the Global Oral Health Action Plan — and during COVID-19 were used to estimate health worker deaths, surge needs for vaccination and inequity in health worker vaccination.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "claude-opus-5-5"
