    {
      "id": "library/9789240081291-eng/blocks/prose-sec-012",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "The underpinning framework",
      "pageStart": 21,
      "pageEnd": 22,
      "target": "../sections/sec-012-the-underpinning-framework.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The concept of the NHWA is closely aligned with the HLM framework for UHC (41) (Figure 3), \nwhich also takes a central place in the High-Level Commission’s report ‘Working for health \nand growth: investing in the health workforce’ (12). This framework provides a comprehensive \npicture of the education sector and HLM dynamics, in which the economy, population and \nsociety act as drivers to attaining UHC and optimal health-care services as the outcome. In \nthis sense, the HLM framework offers tangible directions for countries in terms of HWF policies \nthat may contribute to achieving the GSHRH m",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "The underpinning framework: NHWA is aligned with the health labour market framework for UHC (Figure 3), in which economy, population and societal drivers shape the education sector and labour market dynamics, with UHC and quality services as the outcome. It names four policy groups: on production (infrastructure, enrolment, student selection, teaching staff); on inflows and outflows (migration, attracting unemployed workers, bringing workers back); on maldistribution and inefficiencies (productivity, skill mix, retention in underserved areas); and to regulate the private sector (dual practice, training quality, service delivery). The first three follow an input–throughput–output sequence; the fourth spans the whole labour market. Annex 1 describes how NHWA was developed and revised.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "claude-opus-5-5"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-31"
      }
