    {
      "id": "library/9789240081291-eng/blocks/prose-sec-013",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "A modular approach",
      "pageStart": 22,
      "pageEnd": 25,
      "target": "../sections/sec-013-a-modular-approach.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The NHWA contains a set of 29 indicators, spread over 4 modules, that aim to support national-\nlevel HWF policies to progress towards UHC and SDGs (Figure 4).\n9\nPart I: National Health Workforce Accounts in context\nFigure 4: Overview of labour market components supported by the \nNHWA modules\nFinance and expenditures\n(Module 3)\nStock and flow\n(Module 1)\nWorking conditions, governance and leadership\n(Module 4)\nEducation\n(Module 2)\nThe aim and content of each of the 4 modules are described in more detail below. Module 1 is \nthe key starting point to the NHWA framework, as it collects the most cru",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "A modular approach: NHWA has 29 indicators in 4 modules (Figure 4), with Module 1 as the key starting point. Module 1, stock and flow, covers density, distribution by age, sex, sector and facility type, subnational distribution (for the Global Strategy milestone of halving inequalities in access to a health worker by 2030), foreign-trained and foreign-born workers (for halving dependency on foreign-trained professionals via the WHO Global Code), inflows, voluntary and involuntary outflows, and vacancies. Module 2, education, covers capacity, applications, enrolments and graduations, aligned with SDGs 4 and 5, plus accreditation and standards (milestone 4, by 2020). Module 3, finance and expenditure, maps workforce spending and remuneration including private earnings, informing investment and budget negotiation and SDGs 8 and 5. Module 4 covers labour regulations and policies (decent work, SDG 8), governance and leadership capacity through a central HRH unit, and HRH information system capacity to monitor planning metrics and global frameworks such as the WHO Global Code and the IHR. Every indicator has a standard descriptive page.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "claude-opus-5-5"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-31"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789240081291-eng/blocks/prose-sec-014",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Core principles of NHWA",
      "pageStart": 25,
      "pageEnd": 27,
      "target": "../sections/sec-014-core-principles-of-nhwa.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The NWHA is beyond just a list of indicators; it is a system-strengthening approach to improve \nthe collection, collation, analysis and use of HWF data and enable evidence-informed HWF \nplanning and policy-making. By emphasizing national needs and interests, the NHWA keeps \ncountries at the heart of the process and allows them to define the pace for progressive \nimplementation. An inclusive multi-sectoral and multi-stakeholder governance mechanism at \nnational level is critical for the implementation of the NHWA. By bringing different stakeholders \ntogether, it is possible to identify diverse",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Core principles of NHWA: NHWA is a system-strengthening approach, not just an indicator list, keeping countries at the centre and letting them set the pace. An inclusive multi-sectoral, multi-stakeholder national governance mechanism is critical, helping to identify data sources, standardize collection, build partnerships, produce relevant analyses, drive data use and advocate for resources. Box 2 lists the principles: multi-stakeholder governance, system strengthening, progressive self-paced implementation, a parsimonious set of validated indicators or proxies, a country-owned process led by focal points, continuous updating from diverse existing sources with no primary data collection, and annual data release.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "claude-opus-5-5"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-31"
      }
