    {
      "id": "library/9789240081291-eng/blocks/prose-sec-017",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Methods for computing indicators",
      "pageStart": 29,
      "pageEnd": 35,
      "target": "../sections/sec-017-methods-for-computing-indicators.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Computation of densities\nThe density of HCWs is expressed per 10 000 population. It uses the stock of health workers \ndivided by the size of the population multiplied by 10 000. The definition of the stock can vary \nand the preferred stock statistic is the number of practicing workers of the relevant occupation, \ni.e. those who are directly providing services to or for patients and communities, for which they \nreceived the corresponding training. When data with this definition are not available, then the \nalternative could be to use statistics on professionally active HCWs. These include pract",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Methods for computing indicators. Densities are health workers per 10,000 population, preferably using practising workers, otherwise professionally active, otherwise licensed workers (which may overestimate the workforce); global reporting uses UN World Population Prospects mid-year estimates. Distribution indicators must report all disaggregates and compute shares only from them, since mixing sources for numerator and total creates inconsistencies. Four indicators are composite scores: yes = 1, partial = 0.5, no = 0, scaled to 0–5 when at least 4 sub-indicators are reported, then classed as Sustainable (4.5–5), Well-developed (3.5–4.4), Moderate (2.5–3.4), Limited (1.5–2.4) or Nascent (<1.5), as in WHO SCORE; a worked example yields 3.75, and occupation-level answers are first averaged within a sub-indicator. Measurement is annual, as cross-sectional (31 December), cumulative (1 January–31 December) or cohort flows, with inflow rates using the end-of-year stock and outflow rates the start-of-year stock. Disaggregations written with a comma are independent and with 'and' joint, implemented progressively from the simplest; subnational levels follow the first or second administrative level. Financial data are converted to US$ at UN Treasury mid-year rates; focal points report only numerators for densities; occupations follow ISCO-08 (Annex 2), ideally at 4-digit level, and include personal care and health management and support workers; deviations from definitions should be reported with their methodology.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "claude-opus-5-5"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-31"
      }
