      "content": "Bridging table \nThe following table shows how indicators from NHWA version 1 are classified in NHWA \nversion 2. The status explains if the indicator is maintained as in version 1, merged with another \nindicator, modified substantially, or dropped.\nNHWA V1  \nIndicator \nID\nV1 Indicator  \nabbreviated  \nname\nStatus\nNHWA V2 \nIndicator \nID\nV2 Indicator abbreviated \nname\nModule 1 – Active health workforce stock\n01-01\nHealth worker density\nMaintained\n1-01\nHealth worker density\n01-02\nHealth worker density at \nsubnational level\nMaintained\n1-02\nHealth worker density at \nsubnational level\n01-03\nHealth wor",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Bridging table mapping each of the 78 NHWA version 1 indicators, across v1's ten modules (active stock; education and training; education regulation and accreditation; education finances; labour market flows; employment and working conditions; spending and remuneration; skill mix for models of care; governance and policies; information systems), to its version 2 indicator, with a status of maintained, merged, modified or dropped. Most stock indicators carry over to 1-01 to 1-08; the v1 regulation and accreditation standards, CPD and in-service training fold into 2-07; inflow and exit rates merge into 1-09 and 1-10; the working-hours, wage, social-protection, dual-practice, compulsory-service and attack-prevention regulations merge into 4-01, as does advanced nursing roles; facility-setting shares merge into 1-06 and specialist surgical and family medicine workforce into 1-01; the governance items merge into 4-02; IHR human resources becomes 4-04; and the HRHIS items, and the gender wage gap (modified), go into 4-05. Applications, admissions, drop-out and graduation rates are modified into 2-02 to 2-04. Seventeen rows are marked dropped, among them share of workers across health and social sectors, the master list of accredited institutions, student–educator ratio, total higher-education and in-service training expenditure, unemployment rate, standard working hours, attacks on the health-care system, total and ODA workforce expenditure, public-sector wage ceilings and applied epidemiology training.",
