      "target": "../sections/sec-050-4-01-labour-regulations-and-policies-for-health.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "4 – 02\t Health workforce governance and leadership capacity\n4 – 03\t Share of women in leadership role\n4 – 04\t International Health Regulations implementation capacity\n4 – 05\t National capacity to monitor key metrics for health workforce  \n\t\nplanning and global monitoring frameworks\nNational health workforce accounts: a handbook – second edition\n68\nAbbreviated name\nLabour regulations and policies for health workforce\n4– 01\nIndicator name\nExistence of labour regulations and policies on the employment characteristics, protection  \nand working conditions of the health workforce\nDefinition\nThis ind",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Lists Module 4 indicators 4-02 to 4-05 and gives the metadata sheet for indicator 4-01, labour regulations and policies for health workforce: a composite of twelve self-assessed sub-indicators, scored on a scale from 1 (nascent) to 5 (sustainable), mainly about protecting and safeguarding health and care workers, each with guiding questions. They cover laws on working hours and conditions (maximum days, premiums for night and overtime work, night-work rules, paid leave), minimum wage and its indexation, social protection (maternity, parental and carer leave, childcare, training leave), dual practice in its separate, parallel and in-facility forms, compulsory service with or without incentives, prevention of workplace violence and attacks, mental well-being care packages, in-kind remuneration for rural retention, health worker safety, oversight of private-sector health workers, CHW remuneration (Yes for a fixed salary, Partial for activity-based monetary incentives, No for non-monetary or no pay, Not applicable without CHWs) and advanced nursing roles such as nurse practitioners. Reported every three years for the Global Strategy, Working for Health, SDNM, the Health and Care Worker Compact and CHW guidance, from laws and regulations, social security and legislative records, expert surveys and policy documents.",
