      "target": "../sections/sec-051-4-02-health-workforce-governance-and-leadership.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "71\nIndicator name\nExistence of health workforce governance and leadership capacity at national  \nand/or subnational levels\nDefinition\nThis indicator is a composite of five sub-indicators, see below. The response to these five  \nsub-indicators is aggregated as a score (see part II on methods) on a scale from 1 (nascent)  \nto 5 (sustainable).\nEach sub-indicator is a self-assessment of one aspect of countries’ governance and leadership \ncapacity vis-à-vis of health and care workers. To help answer these self-assessed sub-indicators,  \na series of guiding questions are provided.\nSub-indicator \n4 –",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Metadata sheet for indicator 4-02, health workforce governance and leadership capacity: a composite of five self-assessed sub-indicators, scored on a scale from 1 (nascent) to 5 (sustainable), each with guiding questions — an institutional mechanism or body coordinating an intersectoral workforce agenda approved across ministries (4-02.1); a health workforce unit in the Ministry of Health that develops and monitors workforce policies and plans (4-02.2); mechanisms and models for workforce planning, such as a national HRH planning committee, a planning methodology and complete data (4-02.3); national health workforce education plans aligned with the national health plan and workforce strategy (4-02.4); and institutional models for assessing staffing needs and workload in facilities (4-02.5). Reported every three years for the Global Strategy, Working for Health and SDNM, from ministries of health, education and labour at national and subnational level, workforce policy units, educational institutions and health facilities.",
