      "target": "../sections/sec-052-4-03-share-of-women-in-leadership-role.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "73\nIndicator name\nShare of women in senior management positions in the Ministry of Health\nNumerator\nNumber of women in leadership roles in the Ministry of Health, defined in headcounts\nDenominator\nTotal number of men and women in leadership roles in the Ministry of Health, defined in headcounts\nDefinition\nShare of women, expressed as a percentage, of women in senior management positions in  \nthe Ministry of Health.\nTo monitor and report on this indicator, it is advised to count all individuals at director-level \nor equivalent positions within the Ministry of Health. Using the organigram of the",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Metadata sheets for three Module 4 indicators. 4-03, share of women in leadership role: the percentage of women among director-level or equivalent senior managers in the Ministry of Health, estimable from its organigram or, alternatively, from the sex make-up of World Health Assembly delegations; reported every three years. 4-04, IHR implementation capacity: based on Joint External Evaluation (third edition) indicator D3.2, said to be similar to indicator C6.1 of the State Party Self-assessment Annual Reporting tool (abbreviated 'SAPR' here and 'SPAR' in the reporting frameworks of the same sheet), rated on five levels from no capacity (1) through limited, developed and demonstrated to sustainable capacity (5), according to whether human resources exist in the relevant sectors and at which levels to detect, assess, notify, report and respond to events under the IHR; reported every three years from the Ministry of Health and public health institutions. The block then opens 4-05, national capacity to monitor key metrics: nine self-assessed sub-indicators scored from 1 (nascent) to 5 (sustainable), on whether the HIS reports HRH stock data and whether the HRHIS can report for the IHR and the WHO Global Code of Practice, on education outputs (including a geocoded, regularly updated master list of accredited institutions), entrants, active stock and exits, geocoded facility locations, and the gender pay gap.",
