    {
      "id": "library/9789240081291-eng/blocks/prose-sec-055",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "HWF planning",
      "pageStart": 93,
      "pageEnd": 94,
      "target": "../sections/sec-055-hwf-planning.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "HWF planning is a requirement to strengthen PHC and UHC (9). The development of HWF \nnational plan or other specific health plan can only be achieved if the leadership and governance \ncapacity is available, building on a solid situation analysis that allows planners to identify gaps \nand trends. This includes the national coordination of multiple sectors (e.g, health, education, \nemployment, finance, decentralization, social protection). HWF planning would in principle \nrequire most of the indicators from the present handbook, particularly if an analysis of the \nstock and trend is envisaged. T",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "HWF planning: workforce planning is required to strengthen PHC and UHC, and a national workforce plan needs leadership and governance capacity, a solid situation analysis of gaps and trends, and coordination across sectors such as health, education, employment, finance, decentralization and social protection. In principle it uses most of the handbook's indicators, integrating stock, flows, migration, ageing, education and financing. A table picks six: health worker density (1-01) for current staffing, growth and fit with population need, annual inflows (1-09) and outflows (1-10) for workers entering and leaving the labour market, and governance and leadership capacity (4-02), IHR implementation capacity (4-04) and capacity to monitor key metrics (4-05) to judge whether the systems needed for planning are in place.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "claude-opus-5-5"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-31"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789240081291-eng/blocks/prose-sec-056",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Equity and subnational distribution",
      "pageStart": 94,
      "pageEnd": 96,
      "target": "../sections/sec-056-equity-and-subnational-distribution.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Equity and subnational distribution\nThere is globally a major inequity in the distribution of HCWs (3). This inequity is also present at \nsubnational level of countries, and poses an even bigger problem in countries with already low \ndensity of health workers (25). Several factors can explain these inequities, such as differences \nin salaries across countries that could influence migration of workers, absence of education \ntargeting rural areas’ needs, or the absence of retention mechanism to stimulate the installation \nof HCWs in rural and hard-to-reach areas. To ensure equitable distribution",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Equity and subnational distribution: health and care workers are inequitably distributed globally and within countries, worst where density is already low, owing to factors such as salary differences driving migration, education not targeted at rural needs and no mechanisms to retain workers in rural and hard-to-reach areas. Planners need data on worker numbers and on demand from workload statistics, which facility-level studies using tools such as WISN can supply. Suggested indicators: density (1-01) for comparisons and trends across countries, income groups and subregions; subnational density of specialists (1-02) for gaps and redeployment; distribution by facility type (1-06); inflow trends of foreign-born or foreign-trained workers (1-09) for migration analysis; standards for social accountability and social determinants of health (2-07.1, 2-07.2) so education accounts for rural needs; entry-level wages (3-02) for earnings differences; and in-kind remuneration for rural retention (4-01.8).",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "claude-opus-5-5"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-31"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789240081291-eng/blocks/prose-sec-057",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Retention",
      "pageStart": 96,
      "pageEnd": 97,
      "target": "../sections/sec-057-retention.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Retention\nThe progress in reducing subnational inequities mentioned above relies on the capacity of \ncountries to retain HCWs in rural, remote and other underserved areas. Retention challenges \ncan be observed in the HLM when a misalignment exists with two phenomena occurring at the \nsame time: unemployment of HCWs while several jobs are available and posts remain vacant. \nIncentives are sometimes used as a mechanism to improve retention of HCWs in rural or hard-\nto-reach areas.\nThe table below suggests a series of indicators that can help monitoring retention challenges \nin countries.\nIndicat",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Retention: reducing subnational inequity depends on retaining health and care workers in rural, remote and underserved areas. Retention problems show as a labour market mismatch in which workers are unemployed while jobs exist and posts stay vacant, and incentives are sometimes used to improve rural retention. Suggested indicators: density by occupation and activity level (1-01), contrasting the licensed with the practising workforce; vacancy rate (1-11) by area and occupation and its effect on facilities' PHC capacity; all labour regulation sub-indicators (4-01) on retention, dual practice, health and safety and working conditions; and subnational governance capacity (4-02), on whether decentralized HRH units can track careers, analyse current and future staffing needs, propose redeployment and retention strategies and support central policy-making and resource mobilization.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "claude-opus-5-5"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-31"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789240081291-eng/blocks/prose-sec-058",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Migration",
      "pageStart": 97,
      "pageEnd": 99,
      "target": "../sections/sec-058-migration.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The migration of HCWs is a policy question affecting most countries, either because of their \nreliance on foreign-trained HCWs or because a large proportion of their HWF emigrates. \nThe WHO Global Code of Practice on the International Recruitment of Health Personnel is \nmonitored through the National Reporting Instrument (NRI). Since the launch of NHWA in 2017, \nquantitative indicators included in the NRI are part of the NHWA indicators.\nApproximately 15% of HCWs globally are working outside their country of birth or first \nprofessional qualification, and this varies by Region and by occupatio",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Migration: migration affects most countries, through reliance on foreign-trained workers or large-scale emigration. The WHO Global Code of Practice on international recruitment is monitored through the National Reporting Instrument, whose quantitative indicators have been NHWA indicators since 2017. About 15% of health and care workers globally work outside their country of birth or first qualification, varying by region and occupation; historic south-to-north patterns persist, but the line between source and destination countries is blurring. Global Strategy milestone 3 tracks progress towards halving reliance on foreign-trained professionals, and the density of selected workers, with the UHC service coverage index, determines inclusion on the WHO health workforce Support and Safeguards List. Suggested indicators: density of doctors, nurses, midwives, dentists and pharmacists (1-01, an SSL criterion), distribution by place of birth (1-07) and of training and country of training (1-08), inflows by place of training (1-09), and HRHIS ability to report on the Code and track entrants and exits (4-05.3, 4-05.5, 4-05.7).",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "claude-opus-5-5"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-31"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789240081291-eng/blocks/prose-sec-059",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Gender",
      "pageStart": 99,
      "pageEnd": 123,
      "target": "../sections/sec-059-gender.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "One feature that characterizes the health and care sector in many countries is that it is a highly \nfeminized sector. Across the world women represent 67% of wage workers in the health and \ncare sector (103). One widespread feature of the health and care sector is a significant degree of \noccupational segregation by gender, both across health and care occupations and within them. \nData is needed to better understand these phenomena. Notably, in addition women across the \nsector are often paid less for work of equal value. Women are paid an average of 76 cents for \nevery dollar men are paid in",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Gender section of Part IV, followed by the handbook's Glossary and References. The health and care sector is highly feminized — women are 67% of its wage workers — with marked occupational segregation by gender and women paid on average 76 cents per dollar paid to men; education can also produce gender imbalances. Suggested indicators: distribution by age and sex (1-03) and by sex (1-04), inflows (1-09) and outflows (1-10) by sex, applications, enrolments and graduates by sex (2-02 to 2-04), women in Ministry of Health leadership (4-03) and HRHIS ability to monitor the gender pay gap (4-05.9); a note explains that sex is the disaggregation used because information systems record it, but serves here as a proxy for gender as a social identity. The Glossary defines the terms the indicator sheets flag, among them accreditation, active health worker and the three activity levels (practising, professionally active, licensed to practise), age groups, CHWs, CPD, contract type, dual practice, the six System of Health Accounts facility types (HP.1–HP.6), SNA-based ownership types, gender (socially constructed) and sex (observed at birth), public and private education institutions (classed by management, not funding), the IHR, interprofessional education, job vacancy and vacancy rate, leadership role, place of birth and of training, the public health workforce, remuneration, social accountability, social determinants of health, status in employment, subnational level (first or second administrative level), and total expenditure on health workforce education, defined there as current and capital spending as a percentage of gross national income. The References list 115 numbered sources, chiefly WHO, World Health Assembly, ILO, OECD, UNESCO and UN documents and journal articles, mostly accessed July–August 2023.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "claude-opus-5-5"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-31"
      }
