    {
      "id": "library/9789240081291-eng/blocks/prose-sec-060",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "+ Annex 1: Development of the NHWA and its revision",
      "pageStart": 123,
      "pageEnd": 127,
      "target": "../sections/sec-060-annex-1-development-of-the-nhwa-and-its-revision.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "NHWA and its revision\nNational health workforce accounts: a handbook – second edition\n110\nThe NHWA was officially launched as the data sharing mechanism for Member States at the \nFourth Global Forum on Human Resources for Health, in Dublin 2017. NHWA serves to address \nthe World Health Assembly Resolution WHA69.19, which urged Member States to “implement \npolicy options towards consolidating a core set of human resources for health (HRH) data […] \nto support national policy and planning”.\nThe NHWA, in particular the version 1 indicators, were developed through a stepwise process \nthat included",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Annex 1, Development of the NHWA and its revision: NHWA was launched as a data-sharing mechanism for Member States at the Fourth Global Forum on Human Resources for Health in Dublin in 2017, answering resolution WHA69.19's call for a core set of HRH data. For version 1, over 450 indicators gathered in October 2015 were filtered to 255, rated in a November 2015 Delphi consultation of over 70 experts on relevance, availability and use, and settled by a Technical Advisory Group in workshops at WHO headquarters; WHO also produced an implementation guide. Five years of implementation greatly increased country-validated workforce data and supported national labour market studies, while WHO documented feedback on feasibility and relevance through workshops, stakeholder meetings and roadmaps. A 2022 review found the implementation principles still valid and focused on streamlining indicators: the Health Workforce Department's data unit assessed reporting rates and proposed which indicators to keep, revise or reject; focal points in all six WHO regions and all regional offices responded to online surveys; WHO experts were interviewed; and a technical expert group, in a two-day workshop, recommended retaining, rejecting, modifying or merging indicators, adding new ones and improving implementation. NHWA will keep being reviewed and updated. The annex names version 1 contributors, the Technical Advisory Group, Delphi participants, and funders (the governments of Germany, Ireland, Japan and Norway and USAID).",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "claude-opus-5-5"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-31"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789240081291-eng/blocks/prose-sec-061",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "+ Annex 2: List of occupations covered by the NHWA",
      "pageStart": 127,
      "pageEnd": 132,
      "target": "../sections/sec-061-annex-2-list-of-occupations-covered-by-the-nhwa.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "covered by the NHWA\nNational health workforce accounts: a handbook – second edition\n114\nIn order to provide a comprehensive overview of the HWF, most of the quantitative indicators \nin the NHWA are disaggregated by occupation. Although various classifications for occupations \nmay exist at national level, it is strongly recommended that all data is mapped to the globally \naccepted ISCO-08, when sharing with WHO.\nGiven that the health occupations listed in ISCO-08 do not present the level of granularity \ndesired for national level HWF planning, all countries are encouraged to develop their own",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Annex 2, List of occupations covered by the NHWA: most quantitative indicators are disaggregated by occupation, and data shared with WHO should be mapped to ISCO-08, although countries are encouraged to keep finer national classifications. The NHWA data platform keeps ISCO-08 while adding occupations from the OECD/Eurostat/WHO-Europe joint questionnaire and globally relevant ones not listed in ISCO-08. A table gives 41 occupational groups with ISCO-08 codes where they exist and marks which of modules 1–4 accept data for each — among them medical doctors (generalists 2211; specialists 2212, split into paediatrics, obstetrics and gynaecology, psychiatry, medical, surgical and other), nursing (2221, 3221) and midwifery (2222, 3222) professionals and associates, dentists, pharmacists, laboratory, imaging and pharmaceutical technicians, paramedical practitioners, traditional and complementary medicine practitioners, community health workers (3253), personal care workers in health services (532), allied therapists, social workers, psychologists, biomedical engineers, managerial, administrative, health-information, maintenance and support staff, and epidemiologists including FETP graduates and field epidemiologists. It closes with three sources on classifying health workers, ISCO-08, and HRH monitoring and evaluation.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "claude-opus-5-5"
